\subsection{GEE Decomposition and Confound Structure}
\label{sec:direction}

Direction analysis reveals partial confounding between style and original quality across axes; GEE logistic decomposition disentangles the two effects (full direction-asymmetry analysis in Appendix~\ref{app:direction}).
After controlling for original preference, the formality effect remains significant for both judges (GPT-4o: $\text{OR} = 2.21$, $p = 0.010$; Qwen3-32B: $\text{OR} = 4.20$, $p = 0.0002$; Table~\ref{tab:gee}), with the cross-judge magnitude gap consistent with evaluator-producer bias (\S\ref{sec:limitations}).

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\small
\caption{GEE logistic decomposition (exchangeable correlation) into style and originality effects, ordered by confound structure. Verbosity is confound-free; register is originality-dominated.}
\label{tab:gee}
\begin{tabular}{lcccc}
\toprule
 & \multicolumn{2}{c}{Style OR} & \multicolumn{2}{c}{Originality OR} \\
\cmidrule(lr){2-3} \cmidrule(lr){4-5}
Config & OR [95\% CI] & $p$ & OR [95\% CI] & $p$ \\
\midrule
Qwen3 verb.\ & 13.10 [5.62, 30.55] & ${<}.0001$ & 0.49 [0.21, 1.13] & .096 \\
Qwen3 form.\ & 4.20 [1.99, 8.87] & .0002 & 4.70 [2.22, 9.96] & ${<}.0001$ \\
GPT-4o form.\ & 2.21 [1.21, 4.04] & .010 & 1.82 [0.99, 3.32] & .052 \\
Qwen3 reg.\ & 1.91 [1.01, 3.58] & .046 & 5.72 [3.02, 10.82] & ${<}.0001$ \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

GEE decomposition reveals a confound gradient across axes: verbosity exhibits a pure style bias ($\text{OR} = 13.10$, originality $\text{OR} = 0.49$, $p = .096$), formality shows mixed style and originality effects of comparable magnitude, while register is dominated by originality anchoring ($\text{OR} = 5.72$).
This gradient demonstrates the diagnostic value of per-axis decomposition---a single ``style bias'' metric would obscure these qualitatively different confound structures.

